messages:
sam_support: Heads-up — we're getting two separate customer reports about Safari (iOS & macOS) dropping SSE streams when our widget is loaded inside a 3rd-party iframe. Symptoms: partial events, then no more data and no reconnect. Happened after a deploy but maybe unrelated. Anyone seen this pattern?

eli_eng: Saw something similar last quarter but that was due to a CDN 304 edge revalidate + Safari behaviour. Do we have sample request/response from one of the complains?

sam_support: Pasted one from customer A (redacted):

Request: GET /v1/stream?id=widget-123
Headers: Host: api.redwood.test
  Authorization: Bearer <short-lived-token>
  Accept: text/event-stream
  Origin: https://partner.example.com

Server responses we logged: first ~30s of event chunks, then a 304 from the edge, after which Safari stops rendering further chunks. Chrome and Firefox keep streaming.

sasha_frontend: Safari has weird heuristics for handling 304s on chunked responses. If the edge returns 304 and the connection isn't a full 200 streaming payload, Safari sometimes decides the stream is complete. Also polyfills that fall back to XHR can break if the server expects true chunked transfer.

eli_eng: Can you paste the raw response headers from the edge + origin? key suspects: Cache-Control, Transfer-Encoding, Content-Encoding (gzip), Vary, and any edge-level 304 behaviour.

sam_support: Got them:

Edge -> client (304):
  HTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified
  Date: Tue, 03 May 2026 15:12:34 GMT
  Age: 12
  Via: 1.1 cf-edge
  Server: cloudflare

Origin -> edge (previous 200 stream):
  HTTP/1.1 200 OK
  Content-Type: text/event-stream; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: private, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Transfer-Encoding: chunked
  Connection: keep-alive
  Content-Encoding: gzip

sam_support: customer reported that when we add a query param with a fresh token it reconnects immediately, so sounds auth/token + caching related.

sasha_frontend: Two paths here: 1) EventSource directly (no auth header possible), customers sometimes use token-in-query which works. 2) Polyfill (EventSource that uses XHR/fetch under the hood) which can include Authorization header — but Safari's streaming XHR support is flaky. If edge serves a 304 for the resource, the polyfill will treat that as end-of-stream.

devops_miguel: TL;DR: Cloudflare/edge returning 304 for SSE is unsafe. We should ensure the edge never caches SSE endpoints and never returns 304. Also disable gzip for SSE and set proper cache headers. Suggested origin headers for streaming endpoints: 

  Content-Type: text/event-stream; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate, max-age=0
  Pragma: no-cache
  Vary: Origin
  Connection: keep-alive
  Transfer-Encoding: chunked
  Content-Encoding: identity

eli_eng: +1. Also important: add Vary: Authorization if any edge caches responses based on auth, and/or use a cache-bypass for Authorization-bearing streams. If the CDN caches per-URL without Vary you get cross-requests mismatch and 304s.

sasha_frontend: Quick client workaround to ship to affected customers: add a small heartbeat & reconnection shim inside the host page that detects no events after N seconds and forces a reconnect. For iframe-based widgets you can also postMessage from the iframe to the host to confirm liveness. Example shim: 

  // pseudo
  let last = Date.now();
  const es = new EventSource(url);
  es.onmessage = (ev) => { last = Date.now(); handle(ev); };
  setInterval(() => {
    if (Date.now() - last > 12_000) {
      es.close();
      // exponential backoff reconnect
      setTimeout(() => { es = new EventSource(url); }, 500);
    }
  }, 4000);

sam_support: We also have a customer that can't change widget code (embedded by partners). They asked if we can change server-side to avoid the issue. Actionable fixes?

devops_miguel: For server-side: ensure no 304s for SSE endpoints. On Fastly/Cloudflare set the route to bypass cache (Page Rule / Worker). Set header `Cache-Control: private, no-store` or use `Surrogate-Control: no-store` depending on CDN. Also explicitly remove gzip `Content-Encoding` for SSE responses so Safari doesn't buffer until gzip block completes.

eli_eng: Repro steps we used in staging: run a tiny server that streams every 500ms, front it with Cloudflare worker that returns 304 for an If-Modified-Since check, and then test in Safari — you'll see the same stall. Repro snippet: 

  # server (node) - streams SSE
  app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
    res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
    res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate');
    res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
    res.setHeader('Transfer-Encoding', 'chunked');
    res.flushHeaders();
    let i=0;
    const t = setInterval(() => {
      res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({i, ts: Date.now()})}\n\n`);
      i++;
      if (i>200) { clearInterval(t); res.end(); }
    }, 500);
  });

eli_eng: Then put an edge rule that replies 304 for some validation header and test Safari in an iframe. Works consistently.

sam_support: Ok plan for support rollout: 1) Tell affected customers to use token-in-query or top-level hosting if possible. 2) For us, patch origin to set Cache-Control no-store & disable gzip for SSE paths + add CDN rule to bypass caches. 3) Add a short-term client shim snippet in our public widget docs for partners. 4) Track impacted accounts and offer priority assist.

sasha_frontend: I'll draft the widget shim and docs. Miguel can we get the CDN bypass rule scoped to /v1/stream*?

devops_miguel: Yep I can add worker rule tomorrow. I'll also add tests to CI to ensure SSE endpoints never return 304 in our staging fronting layer.

sam_support: Customer-facing note ready: "We're mitigating an edge-caching interaction that can cause Safari embedded widgets to stop receiving events. Temporary workaround: append a rotating param to the stream URL or host the widget top-level. We're deploying a server-side fix to bypass CDNs for streaming endpoints." :thumbsup:

